.TH "CSSMATHNEGATE" "3JS" "August 11, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
CSSMathNegate \- CSSMathNegate
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]CSSMathNegate\f[B]\f[R] interface of the CSS Typed Object
Model API represents the negation of a \f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R].
.SH CONSTRUCTOR
.TP
\f[B]CSSMathNegate()\f[R]
Creates a new \f[CR]CSSMathNegate\f[R] object.
.SH INSTANCE PROPERTIES
\f[I]Also inherits properties from its parent interface,
\f[CI]CSSMathValue\f[I].\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]CSSMathNegate.value\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R]
Returns a \f[CR]CSSNumericValue\f[R] object.
.SH STATIC METHODS
\f[I]Also inherits methods from its parent interface,
\f[CI]CSSMathValue\f[I].\f[R]
.SH INSTANCE METHODS
\f[I]Also inherits methods from its parent interface,
\f[CI]CSSMathValue\f[I].\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[CR]CSSMathNegate\f[R] corresponds to applying unary minus to a
numeric value (\f[CR]x\f[R] becomes \f[CR]\-x\f[R]).
.PP
Generally you won\(cqt construct a \f[CR]CSSMathNegate\f[R] directly.
It\(cqs produced by the internal negation step that \f[CR]sub()\f[R]
applies to its arguments before adding them: negating a
\f[CR]CSSMathSum\f[R], \f[CR]CSSMathProduct\f[R], \f[CR]CSSMathMin\f[R],
\f[CR]CSSMathMax\f[R], \f[CR]CSSMathClamp\f[R], or
\f[CR]CSSMathInvert\f[R] wraps it in a \f[CR]CSSMathNegate\f[R].
Negating a plain \f[CR]CSSUnitValue\f[R] (a length, percentage, etc.)
instead flips the sign of its \f[CR]value\f[R] directly, so subtracting
simple values doesn\(cqt normally produce a \f[CR]CSSMathNegate\f[R].
.PP
A \f[CR]CSSMathNegate\f[R] can also appear when reading a computed
value: for example, \f[CR]get()\f[R] on a property set with a
\f[CR]calc()\f[R] expression that subtracts a value returns a
\f[CR]CSSMathSum\f[R] whose operands may include a
\f[CR]CSSMathNegate\f[R].
You can identify it by checking the \f[CR]operator\f[R] property for the
string \f[CR]\(dqnegate\(dq\f[R].
.PP
\f[CR]CSSMathNegate\f[R] serializes using CSS \f[CR]calc()\f[R] syntax,
as \f[CR]calc(\-<value>)\f[R].
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
The following code creates a \f[CR]CSSMathNegate\f[R] object from a
length, then logs the constructor name, \f[CR]value\f[R], and the
object\(cqs serialization (from \f[CR]toString()\f[R]).
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] negated = \f[B]new\f[R] CSSMathNegate(CSS.px(10));

console.log(negated.constructor.name); \f[I]// \(dqCSSMathNegate\(dq\f[R]
console.log(negated.value); \f[I]// CSSUnitValue {value: 10, unit: \(dqpx\(dq}\f[R]
console.log(negated.toString()); \f[I]// \(dqcalc(\-10px)\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.PP
Note that if a plain number is passed to \f[CR]arg\f[R], the
\f[CR]value\f[R] is rectified to a \f[CR]CSSUnitValue\f[R] with unit
\f[CR]\(dqnumber\(dq\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] negatedNumber = \f[B]new\f[R] CSSMathNegate(4);

console.log(negatedNumber.value); \f[I]// CSSUnitValue {value: 4, unit: \(dqnumber\(dq}\f[R]
console.log(negatedNumber.toString()); \f[I]// \(dqcalc(\-4)\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.SS Subtracting a composite value
\f[CR]sub()\f[R] produces a \f[CR]CSSMathNegate\f[R] when the value
being subtracted is itself a composite value, such as a
\f[CR]CSSMathSum\f[R] (rather than a plain \f[CR]CSSUnitValue\f[R]).
.PP
This is demonstrated by the following code.
The \f[CR]px\f[R] and \f[CR]percent\f[R] can\(cqt be combined into a
single value without knowing the containing block size, so the value of
\f[CR]composite\f[R] is represented as a \f[CR]CSSMathSum\f[R].
When this value is subtracted, the value of \f[CR]composite\f[R] is
wrapped in a \f[CR]CSSMathNegate\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] composite = CSS.px(10).add(CSS.percent(5)); \f[I]// CSSMathSum: calc(10px + 5%)\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] result = CSS.px(100).sub(composite);

console.log(result.constructor.name); \f[I]// \(dqCSSMathSum\(dq\f[R]
console.log(result.values[1].constructor.name); \f[I]// \(dqCSSMathNegate\(dq\f[R]
console.log(result.values[1].value); \f[I]// CSSMathSum {values: CSSNumericArray, operator: \(dqsum\(dq}\f[R]
console.log(result.toString()); \f[I]// \(dqcalc(100px \- (10px + 5%))\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.SS Parsing \f[CR]calc()\f[R]
A \f[CR]CSSMathNegate\f[R] can also be created when using
\f[CR]CSSStyleValue.parse()\f[R] to parse a \f[CR]calc()\f[R] expression
that can\(cqt be resolved to a single value.
.PP
For example, in the following code \f[CR]CSSStyleValue.parse()\f[R]
parses a value for the \f[CR]width\f[R] property that subtracts a length
from a percentage (which can\(cqt be combined until layout).
The result is a \f[CR]CSSMathSum\f[R] where the first value in the array
is a \f[CR]CSSUnitValue\f[R], and the second value is a
\f[CR]CSSMathNegate\f[R] object representing negation of the second
operand passed to the \f[CR]calc()\f[R] function.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] width = CSSStyleValue.parse(\(dqwidth\(dq, \(dqcalc(50% \- 10px)\(dq);

console.log(width.constructor.name); \f[I]// \(dqCSSMathSum\(dq\f[R]
console.log(width.values[0]); \f[I]// CSSUnitValue {value: 50, unit: \(aqpercent\(aq}\f[R]
console.log(width.values[1]); \f[I]// CSSMathNegate {value: CSSUnitValue, operator: \(aqnegate\(aq}\f[R]
console.log(width.values[1].value); \f[I]// CSSUnitValue {value: 10, unit: \(dqpx\(dq}\f[R]

console.log(width.toString()); \f[I]// \(dqcalc(50% \- 10px)\(dq\f[R]
.EE
